# FACT · the ghostwheel ring-of-rings (Corwin's architecture note, 2026-08-05)

**NEW INFORMATION, his words in chat, prototype design: 17 nodes** ("because of course it
does"). Three ring classes, quoted near-verbatim with his emphasis kept:

**GREATER RINGS** (more servers, fewer integral rings): *"3 rings of five servers where any
3/5 means available, not more than two being reserved for one each of dev or stage/test at any
given time."*

**LESSER RINGS** (fewer servers, more integral rings): *"five rings of three servers where any
two rings of servers may similarly be dev/stage provided at least one server in the ring dev
or test have a server that has that role per the first composition thus functioning as a
bastien to accept changes across the greater rings feature space (e.g. as/for dev in some
server that is the dev to the greater ring and part of a lesser ring designated for dev)."*
— the BASTION rule: change crosses ring classes only through a host holding the same role in
both compositions.

**EDGE RINGS**: *"the ring composed by taking all hosts that are or are not in service and
irrespective of placement into any rings: the palette for adding and removing hosts, pinning
features to operate only within them, and for building and manipulating the rings;
visualization may be from either (GREATER/LESSOR) ring context but JOURNEY (control) is
effected only from the edge ring perspective which must 'per the shrink wrapping' be able to
walk the edges and prove vs imbalances to plot/perform the 'move' (script or effect actual
changes) driving us toward our run-level gated functionality."*

**The default stance, his (against blind convention, kept whole):** people must drop to the
system-conceptualized run-level to effect new system services — *"by default: we can't control
how people 'optimize' and this defensive careful orchistration at the system level is just the
sort of blind obedience to convention we are creating tools to avoid implicitly assuming in
all the software we make going forward"* — so it may really happen that people innovate safe
service-def changes **without** dropping (big CI/CD feature, *"WAY too hard for me to know how
we can plan for at this stage of our bootstrap"*).

