## Readings (JOURNEY — mine, arguable)

- **The vocabulary is already ours.** Visualization-from-any-context / control-only-from-edge
  is the cube's line exactly: we always SHOW the move-space; effecting moves is gated (WHO,
  run-level, keeper). "Walk the edges and prove vs imbalances, then plot/perform the move" is
  the dungeon walk + the second index + the duplex/debug discipline in infrastructure clothes.
- **The arithmetic:** 2/3 and 3/5 are both smallest-majority-that-survives-the-stated-reserve.
  The lesser ring tolerates one absence; the greater tolerates two (and spends them,
  deliberately, on dev/stage). 17 reads as 15 ringed + 2 edge-spares — flagged as my reading,
  not his statement.
- **Ring-of-rings is the counting ceiling applied to MANY:** 17 nodes is MANY; structured as
  rings of 3 and 5, every local view drops back inside 1-2-3(-5) — navigable without an index
  from inside any single ring, indexed (edge ring = the second index) across them.
- **First tenant:** the ollama access tier's three nodes (gw19, gw17, gw22) are proto-lesser-
  ring `lesser.1`; the walkthrough (h/WALKTHROUGH-2026-08-05-kimi-k3-ollama-ghostwheel.md)
  now writes its health predicate as the ring predicate. The K3 oracle is an EDGE-ring pinned
  feature, not a ring member.
- **The un-plannable feature has a shape here already:** changing service defs without
  dropping run-level = rebasing a live promise without a breakpoint — gen5's stitching/givens
  machinery (journaled exits, no override, consent-mapped authority) is the lab-scale model
  of it. Named as an opening, not built.

## The derivation (HIS, confirmed 2026-08-05 — how people change the magic numbers)

*"Consider the outer level - the edge ring. It has a dev and a test and some number of
servers. According to our basic 'big number' pin of seventeen that has to be 15. Divide that
by our value for MANY (which must be odd to the context, so usually just an odd number which
obvs is three by default) and we get: five servers. Invert and we know the size of the lessor
ring."* — Corwin, chat.

Formalized: **ring topology = f(PIN, RESERVE=2, MANY)** where greater-size = (PIN−RESERVE)/MANY
(count = MANY), lesser-size = MANY (count = greater-size), edge = all PIN hosts. Changing a
magic number re-derives the topology; nothing else is design. Sharpening (mine, confirmed
against his "must be odd to the context"): clean majorities want BOTH factors odd, so valid
pins are (odd×odd)+2 — and **17 is the smallest pin with DISTINCT odd factors** (3×5; 11 = 3×3
degenerates, dual = self). {1, 2, MANY} closes the stream between "unlimited servers!" and
"seventeen servers!": one pin, one reserved pair, one odd divisor.

